'''Pikachu shorts''' are {{aniseries|PTS}}-related shorts that almost exclusively feature {{OBP|Pokémon|species}} and chronicle miscellaneous adventures of [[Ash's Pikachu]].

__TOC__

==Categories==
The shorts can roughly be sorted into the following categories:
* The '''bonus mini-movies''' (Japanese: '''{{ruby|同時上映|どうじじょうえい}}''' ''simultaneous screenings''), which are shorts that aired theatrically together with a corresponding [[Pokémon movie|movie]] in Japan: [[PK01]] with [[M01]], [[PK04]] with [[M02]], [[PK07]] with [[M03]], [[PK10]] with [[M04]], [[PK11]] with [[M05]], [[PK12]] with [[M06]], [[PK24]] with [[M15]], [[PK25]] with [[M16]], [[PK26]] with [[M17]], and [[PK27]] with [[M18]]. The first six shorts are occasionally included on home video releases with their corresponding movies. With the exception of [[PK07]], they were available on [[Netflix]] in Japan under the title '''Pokémon: Short Films Collection''' (Japanese: '''ポケモン{{ruby|映画|えいが}} ショートフィルム・コレクション''' ''Pokémon Movie Short Films Collection'').
* The (Japanese: '''ANA{{ruby|機内上映|きないじょうえい}}''' ''ANA in-flight screenings''), which are shorts which were shown during the month of August on even-numbered {{wp|All Nippon Airways|ANA}} flights: [[PK13]] in 2004, [[PK15]] in 2005, and every Pikachu short from [[PK17]] in 2006 until [[PK22]] in 2011. These shorts were subsequently released on DVD. Only [[PK17]] has been dubbed.
* The three '''[[Pikachu's Winter Vacation]]''' two-episode [[Christmas]] specials: [[PK02]]/[[PK03]] in 1998, [[PK05]]/[[PK06]] in 1999, and [[PK08]]/[[PK09]] in 2000.
* The two (Japanese: '''ポケモン3Dアドベンチャー''' ''Pokémon 3D Adventure'') shorts, which take advantage of 3D cinema technology: [[PK14]] and [[PK16]]. Only the latter has been dubbed.
* The (Japanese: '''スペシャルアニメ''' ''special anime'') short, which was released on a magazine bonus DVD: [[PK23]]. It was later shown on [[Pokémon Smash!]], made available on the official Japanese Pokémon [[YouTube]] channel, and included with the home video release of [[M15]].

